# Hartree-Fock geometry optimization of LiH -- the `gopt` path of the `scf`
# workflow, and the only deck here that optimizes a geometry.
#
# `gopt true` hands the SCF to MolOpt (src/madness/chem/molopt.h), which projects
# translations and rotations out of the gradient before testing convergence.  The
# raw gradient carries a spurious net force of the order of the gradient
# accuracy -- for this deck 1.6e-4 a.u., an identical dE/dz on both atoms -- so
# without that projection `max-g` never reaches `gtol` and the run burns
# `gmaxiter` cycles at a geometry that stopped moving after two.  With it, the
# projected gradient converges to 5e-7.  LiH is linear, so the projector must
# also cope with the vanishing rotation about the molecular axis.
#
# The geometry tolerances are not set here and the `gtol`/`gprec` knobs in the
# `dft` group do not reach this path: Applications.hpp derives them from
# `protocol().back()` (Dalton-style), so the 1e-6 rung is what gives
# gtol = xtol = 1e-5, etol = 2e-6 and a gradient precision of 1e-6.  A single
# 1e-4 rung would instead put gtol at 2e-4 -- above the spurious net force,
# i.e. a criterion that cannot tell a projected gradient from an unprojected
# one.  That is the reason this case pays for the ladder.
#
# The start is 0.015 bohr inside the HF minimum (r_e = 3.0351 bohr), which the
# optimizer closes in two steps.
#
# Run:   ./run.sh          (or: madqc --wf=scf scf_lih_gopt.in)

dft
    xc          hf
    protocol    [1e-4, 1e-6]
    dconv       1e-4
    gopt        true
    gmaxiter    10
end

molecule
    units   atomic
    eprec   1e-6
    Li  0.0  0.0  0.0
    H   0.0  0.0  3.05
end
